{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1778869304,"nonce":"7QfQic1rb+YpaLjZ4Asxl85roFg=","message":"Mandible should not hardcode Feedbax internals. The intended division is:\n\nFeedbax owns:\n\n- Feedbax schemas.\n- Spec validation.\n- Component registry and component extension contracts.\n- Analysis registry and analysis extension contracts.\n- Training/evaluation/analysis/report execution semantics.\n- Studio entry points for Feedbax inspection and editing.\n- Emitted manifests and artifact role semantics.\n\nMandible owns:\n\n- Project and issue/work graphs.\n- Agent sessions and continuation context.\n- Review/auth policies.\n- Provider registry.\n- Artifact storage backends and stable artifact IDs.\n- Provenance graph ingestion and cross-project relationships.\n- Conditional UI actions based on provider capabilities.\n\nThis division should let Mandible show actions such as \"Open in Feedbax Studio\",\n\"Validate training spec\", \"Start evaluation\", or \"Publish report\" only when Feedbax is\ninstalled, healthy, compatible with the selected node/artifact/spec, and permitted by\nreview policy.\n\n\nFeedbax and Feedbax Studio must remain usable without Mandible. A user should still be\nable to:\n\n- Build and edit models/graphs in Studio.\n- Register project components, tasks, losses, protocols, and analyses through Feedbax's\n  own extension mechanisms.\n- Validate specs locally.\n- Launch local or remote training/evaluation/analysis runs through Feedbax/Studio\n  surfaces.\n- Emit manifests and local artifact references.\n- Rebuild Feedbax's local database/indexes from manifests.\n- Inspect runs, artifacts, and analysis/report products inside Feedbax/Studio.\n\nWithout Mandible, the user loses Mandible-specific benefits: cross-repo project memory,\nissue/work graphs, agent session orchestration, review/auth queues, Mandible artifact\nstorage IDs, and Mandible's graph/provenance workspace. They should not lose the core\nFeedbax workbench.\n\nWhen Mandible is absent, Feedbax can use local artifact paths, local content hashes, and\nFeedbax-managed indexes. When Mandible is present, those same manifests can be ingested\nand enriched with Mandible artifact IDs, project graph nodes, issue/session provenance,\nand review metadata.\n\n\nThe provider contract should be portable enough that another tool could act as a\nMandible alternative. An external orchestrator should be able to:\n\n- Discover Feedbax provider capabilities.\n- Fetch schemas and registry snapshots.\n- Validate specs before execution.\n- Start supported executions through Feedbax transports.\n- Receive manifests and artifact references.\n- Store artifacts in its own backend and return stable artifact IDs.\n- Ingest provenance edges into its own project graph or database.\n\nThis is a design constraint on Feedbax: provider capabilities, schemas, manifests, and\nregistry snapshots should not require Mandible-specific APIs or object models. Mandible\ncan be the first and richest orchestrator, but it should not be the only possible client\nof the Feedbax provider contract.\n\n\nStudio should eventually operate over the same manifest and provider concepts:\n\n- The lower pane should be able to show run sets and pipeline collections, not only one\n  active run.\n- Studio-created runs should emit the same manifests as CLI/script runs.\n- Imported runs should be indexable by rebuilding the database from manifests.\n- Studio graph specs should reference registered component type IDs.\n- Studio should distinguish display-only templates from executable components.\n- Studio should surface validation errors before launching training/evaluation.\n\nDetailed Studio UX belongs in separate Studio issues/specs. This spec only sets the data\nand provider contracts those UI decisions should use.\n\n\nA useful first implementation should be small:\n\n1. Define manifest dataclasses/Pydantic models for `TrainingRunSetManifest`,\n   `TrainingRunManifest`, `ArtifactRef`, and shared provenance fields.\n2. Emit a manifest at the end of a local or worker training run.\n3. Hash and reference checkpoint/history artifacts.\n4. Add a simple rebuildable SQLite index for training manifests.\n5. Add a provider capability manifest function or CLI command.\n6. Add a registry entry type with metadata plus builder reference.\n7. Register a small set of built-ins through the new entry type.\n8. Let `spec_to_graph()` consult the registry for those built-ins before hardcoded\n   fallback.\n9. Add registry snapshots for tasks, losses, and analyses even if their first\n   implementations wrap existing `ExperimentRegistry` behavior.\n\nThis is enough to prove the architecture without redesigning all Studio workflows at\nonce.\n\n\nFuture agents should ask for preference before making irreversible or UX-heavy choices,\nespecially:\n\n- Whether manifest files should be one file per object, one file per run set, or a\n  hybrid.\n- Where project-local manifests and artifacts should live by default.\n- Which old database records must be migrated versus treated as legacy/import-only.\n- Which Studio views should remain separate once run sets and manifests become central.\n- How strict to be about refusing durable runs that depend on unreviewed local component\n  code.\n- Whether a proposed component-registry refactor is a straightforward generalization or\n  a dubious change that needs explicit approval.\n\n\n- Should manifest IDs be UUIDs, content-derived IDs, or scoped names plus hashes?\n- What schema/versioning library should be used for long-lived manifest compatibility?\n- How should parameter override paths be represented for nested PyTrees and Equinox\n  modules?\n- How much component shape/dtype information can be validated statically?\n- How should provider schemas be exported: JSON Schema, OpenAPI, Python type metadata,\n  or a combination?\n- How should Mandible artifact IDs be reconciled with Feedbax local artifact paths when\n  runs are created outside Mandible and imported later?\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}